import{_ as t,o as a,a as s,d as n}from"./chunks/framework.IehvnstE.js";const m=JSON.parse('{"title":"Builds","description":"Builds in Stuzhik: the build page, changing the game version with a way back, backups without repeats, game settings and graphics presets, shared shaders, worlds and screenshots, build translation and a Steam shortcut.","frontmatter":{"description":"Builds in Stuzhik: the build page, changing the game version with a way back, backups without repeats, game settings and graphics presets, shared shaders, worlds and screenshots, build translation and a Steam shortcut.","head":[["meta",{"name":"keywords","content":"stuzhik build, change minecraft version, roll back modpack, minecraft backup, minecraft graphics presets, shared shaders, modpack translation, minecraft steam deck"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/guide/instances.md","filePath":"en/guide/instances.md"}'),o={name:"en/guide/instances.md"};function r(i,e,d,h,l,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="builds" tabindex="-1">Builds <a class="header-anchor" href="#builds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Builds&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A build is a separate Minecraft with its own mods, configs and worlds. <strong>Builds don&#39;t affect each other</strong>: one for survival, another for tech mods, a third a server for friends.</p><h2 id="creating" tabindex="-1">Creating <a class="header-anchor" href="#creating" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Creating&quot;">​</a></h2><p>«Create» in the sidebar (a plus while it is collapsed): client or server, the game version and a loader (Fabric, Quilt, Forge, NeoForge or none). A ready-made build can be installed from the modpack catalog, see <a href="/en/guide/modpacks.html">Modpacks</a>.</p><h2 id="the-build-page" tabindex="-1">The build page <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-build-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The build page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>On the left: the name, «Play» and the tabs; on the right: what the tab holds. The numbers on the tabs match what is inside and <strong>change at once, wherever the build changed</strong>: in the launcher, as a file in the folder or through the console version.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tab</th><th>What is there</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mods</td><td>the build&#39;s mods: turn on, update, remove (<a href="/en/guide/mods.html">Mods</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Dependencies</td><td>what needs what, conflicts, mods not for this build</td></tr><tr><td>Modpack</td><td>updating the modpack the build was installed from</td></tr><tr><td>Resources, Shaders, Datapacks</td><td>the build&#39;s packs and shared packs</td></tr><tr><td>Screenshots</td><td>shots from the game</td></tr><tr><td>Analyze Logs</td><td>crash analysis and steps to take (<a href="/en/guide/log-analyzer.html">Log Analyzer</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Backups</td><td>the build&#39;s backups</td></tr><tr><td>Worlds</td><td>the build&#39;s worlds and copying them</td></tr></tbody></table><p>What is needed less often sits under «More»: «Files» (the config editor), «Profiles», «Patches», «Performance» and «Console». A server has tabs of its own: <a href="/en/guide/servers.html">Servers</a>.</p><p>Actions on a build are in its menu (right click on the card or «⋯»): «Game Settings», «Open Folder», «Edit», «Export build», «Change cover», «Add to Steam», «Build translation», «Repair» and others.</p><h2 id="version-change" tabindex="-1">Changing the game version or loader <a class="header-anchor" href="#version-change" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Changing the game version or loader {#version-change}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In «Edit», change the game version or the loader and save. The «Change version» window opens and shows in advance:</p><ul><li><strong>Will update</strong>: mods that have a version for the new game. New dependencies those versions need are installed in the same step</li><li><strong>Will be disabled</strong>: mods without such a version. They stay in the list turned off; you can turn them on or replace them later</li><li><strong>Stay as they are</strong>: mods that fit and pinned ones</li><li>what happens to the worlds: in a newer version they are upgraded on first entry, in an older one blocks and items may go missing</li></ul><p><strong>Before the change a rollback point is saved</strong>: the whole build with its worlds. Until you press «Keep», the build page has «Roll back», which returns the mods, configs, worlds and version as they were.</p><p>When a new stable loader version comes out for the build&#39;s game, the page shows a hint «A new NeoForge is out: …». «Move…» opens the same version change window, «Not now» hides the hint until the next version.</p><h2 id="backups" tabindex="-1">Backups <a class="header-anchor" href="#backups" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Backups {#backups}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>A backup is made by itself</strong> before mods are updated or removed (at most once every 10 minutes) and by «Create backup» on the «Backups» tab. The number of automatic backups is limited; manual ones are never removed.</p><p>Identical files are stored once for all of a build&#39;s backups, so <strong>backups take almost no space</strong>: a backup of an unchanged build is instant, and after a mod update it adds only that mod. You can restore «Without worlds» or «With worlds», and only the files that changed are rewritten.</p><p>If the build has a backup mod (FTB Backups, Simple Backups and the like), the launcher notices and backs up only mods and configs: the mod backs up the worlds.</p><h2 id="game-settings" tabindex="-1">Game settings <a class="header-anchor" href="#game-settings" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Game settings {#game-settings}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>The build&#39;s «Game Settings»</strong> are its Minecraft settings without starting the game, and inside them are graphics presets: a build&#39;s graphics can be saved under a name and applied to any other build. A preset holds the game&#39;s video settings, the chosen shader with its settings and the graphics of mods (Sodium, Iris, Embeddium, Distant Horizons and others). It leaves field of view, brightness, interface scale, controls and sound alone. A preset can be saved to a file and loaded from one.</p><p><strong>Settings → In game → For every build</strong>: render distance, field of view, brightness, volume, mouse sensitivity and the rest. The category with the «In every build» switch is the same everywhere, and what you change in the game comes back to the launcher; the other values go to new builds.</p><p><strong>Settings → In game → Language &amp; hints</strong>: Minecraft&#39;s tutorial hints («Use WASD») and the narrator are off by default, and a new build starts in the system language. All of it comes back with the switches there.</p><p><strong>Settings → Launch → Per-build settings</strong>: which builds have their own memory, Java, arguments or game settings instead of the common ones. One button puts them back to the common ones, for one build or for all.</p><h2 id="shaders-and-resource-packs" tabindex="-1">Shaders and resource packs <a class="header-anchor" href="#shaders-and-resource-packs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Shaders and resource packs&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>A shared pack.</strong> The «Make shared: for every build» button in a shader&#39;s or resource pack&#39;s row moves the file to the shared folder, and it is connected to every build when the game starts. A shared shader&#39;s settings are shared too, but a build&#39;s own shader settings win</li><li><strong>A shader preset.</strong> The «Preset» button of a shader pack shows the profiles the pack&#39;s author defined (for example Low, Medium, High in BSL and Complementary). The launcher changes only the pack&#39;s settings file; your other settings of the pack stay</li></ul><h2 id="screenshots-and-worlds" tabindex="-1">Screenshots and worlds <a class="header-anchor" href="#screenshots-and-worlds" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Screenshots and worlds&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A new screenshot from the game and a new world <strong>appear on their tabs by themselves</strong>, with no «Refresh» button. A screenshot can become the launcher&#39;s background or the build&#39;s cover.</p><p>A world is copied to another build with «Copy to...». If a world with the same name is already there, the copy gets a new one («World (2)») and leaves the other world alone.</p><h2 id="build-translation" tabindex="-1">Build translation <a class="header-anchor" href="#build-translation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build translation {#build-translation}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>«Build translation» in the build&#39;s menu («More…») collects the texts the build carries itself: FTB Quests and Heracles quests, Patchouli books, KubeJS and OpenLoader texts, and shows how much of it is already in Russian.</p><ul><li>«Export strings…»: a file for a translator, «Load translation…»: lay a finished one over the build</li><li>«Take from other versions»: pick up the Russian of mods from other versions of the same mods on Modrinth and CurseForge: only lines with the same English text are taken</li><li>«Remove translation» returns the build&#39;s files exactly as they were</li></ul><p>A translation survives a modpack update and a game version change. When the Stuzhik team has a ready translation of a modpack, it is installed by itself after a build is installed or updated; to choose «Ask» or turn it off: «Settings → Mods».</p><h2 id="steam" tabindex="-1">A build in Steam <a class="header-anchor" href="#steam" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;A build in Steam {#steam}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>«Add to Steam» in the build&#39;s menu adds it to the Steam library as a non-Steam game. It starts from Big Picture and on the Steam Deck <strong>right away, without the launcher&#39;s window</strong>, and Steam sees how long it ran. It has its own cover, tile and logo, made from the build&#39;s cover. An open Steam rewrites its shortcuts when it exits, so the launcher closes and reopens it only if you pressed the button that says so.</p><h2 id="data-location" tabindex="-1">Where the data is <a class="header-anchor" href="#data-location" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Where the data is {#data-location}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The launcher&#39;s builds, worlds, Java, database and logs live in one folder. «Settings → Storage → Location» shows where it is and <strong>moves it to any disk</strong>: on the same disk instantly (the folder is renamed at the restart), to another one by copying with every file checked, after which the old folder is removed.</p><p>Portable mode: put a file <code>portable.txt</code> next to the program, and all the data, the interface settings included, goes into the <code>data</code> folder next to it.</p>',37)])])}const g=t(o,[["render",r]]);export{m as __pageData,g as default};
